---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/alternatives/marsello-alternative";
const title = "Marsello alternatives: preserve Shopify loyalty and POS benefits";
const desc = "Evaluate a Marsello sender change against loyalty balances, refunds, customer identity and POS reward continuity.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Must a new SMS sender replace Marsello loyalty?", "answer": "No. Scope the change first: messaging alone, or loyalty and messaging together. Retaining a working benefits ledger can reduce the number of customer promises that must move at once."}, {"question": "Does a loyalty member have SMS permission?", "answer": "Membership, a points balance and a telephone number do not establish marketing permission. Preserve sender, purpose, channel, disclosure, source, timestamp and current withdrawal records separately from loyalty state."}, {"question": "What should a refund test prove?", "answer": "Under the store\u2019s documented earning and reversal rules, reconcile the original order, refunded product value, points adjustment and any queued reward announcement. Replay must not reverse points twice or announce a benefit that is no longer available."}, {"question": "What makes the replacement ready?", "answer": "Staff can find the right customer and benefit at the till; balances reconcile; withdrawals and purchases suppress pending sends; and each trigger has one queue owner. Readiness comes from these accepted jobs, not an arbitrary overlap period."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Marsello alternatives: keep the benefits customers earned</h1>
      <p>For a store selling online and at the till, replacing a messaging sender is also a customer-service decision if rewards drive those messages. First decide whether to retain Marsello’s loyalty ledger and change delivery, or move the benefits program too. An SMS feature checklist cannot establish that points, redeemed rewards and cashier workflows survive.</p>
      <h2>What Marsello already contributes</h2>
      <p><a href="https://help.marsello.com/how-do-i-connect-my-shopify-site-to-marsello-marsello-help-center">Marsello’s Shopify integration guide</a> describes customer and purchase syncing, with online and POS sites enabled separately. Enabled sites support earning points and redeeming available rewards or discounts. At POS, staff attach the customer to the sale and use the Loyalty tile to inspect available benefits. That continuity is a positive reason to retain a working loyalty setup while evaluating a different sender.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://help.marsello.com/how-do-i-automatically-remove-loyalty-points-for-shopify-orders-marsello-help-center">Shopify points-removal guide</a> ties reversals to configured payment statuses. It describes Paid earning and Refunded reversal defaults; selecting Partially Refunded removes points for refunded product value. Inspect your actual settings rather than assuming every store has those defaults. Sources checked November 25, 2026; the exercises below are proposed acceptance work, not observed merchant results.</p>
      <h2>Choose the boundary before choosing the supplier</h2>
      <p>A messaging-only replacement should accept the loyalty system’s authoritative benefit state without creating a second balance. A full replacement must also demonstrate earning rules, reward redemption, outstanding vouchers, expiry and tier treatment, site coverage and staff access. Ask each candidate to show the job in your account and country; a catalog description or rank cannot prove it.</p>
      <p>Use the <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Klaviyo and retired Yotpo messaging migration guide</a> when the problem is sender reconstruction with retained Reviews or Loyalty. That is a different boundary from moving Marsello’s benefits ledger. The <a href="/alternatives/recart-alternative">Recart alternative guide</a> focuses on capture and cart queues; the <a href="/alternatives/attentive-alternative">Attentive alternative guide</a> covers promotional and service-message handover. None is a default replacement for POS rewards.</p>
      <h2>Proposed fixture: one customer, two sales channels, one ledger</h2>
      <p>Create a test customer with a stable Shopify customer identifier, an online order, an in-store purchase and a reward already redeemed. Record order and reward identifiers, points earned, spent, adjusted and remaining, tier status, expiry and site. Keep a second customer with a similar name and a shared household phone number. Ask a cashier to find the correct account, explain the available benefit and redeem it without merging those people on phone number alone.</p>
      <p>Export a benefits ledger and a separate permission ledger. For each balance, reconcile opening points + earned points − spent points − reversals + manual adjustments = closing points. In a proposed fixture, 500 + 100 − 200 − 40 + 10 = 370 points. This is illustrative arithmetic, not a Marsello earning rule or a measured account balance. Track reward codes separately: a points total does not prove whether a previously issued voucher remains usable.</p>
      <h2>Refunds and benefit announcements must agree</h2>
      <p>Queue a tier or reward message, then partially refund the online order before dispatch. Use the configured reversal rule to calculate the new state and inspect the waiting message: a stale snapshot must not promise an unavailable reward. Repeat the same refund event and then deliver an older paid event. Require one adjustment per source event and a current-state check before the announcement leaves. Test full cancellation, an exchange and a refund after redemption too; write down the store’s treatment of each rather than inventing a universal policy.</p>
      <p>Keep the benefit service active while messaging is paused if your chosen boundary allows it. Staff need a documented route for balance corrections and disputed redemptions, with appropriate account permissions and an audit record. Verify the POS extension and each enabled site on the registers actually used. Define what staff tell a customer if syncing is delayed instead of displaying an unverified balance as current.</p>
      <h2>Permission and queue continuity</h2>
      <p>Preserve checkout, popup and keyword disclosures alongside the sender, purpose, channel, timestamp and source. Carry withdrawals and suppressions through the latest reconciliation; loyalty enrollment and email permission do not authorize SMS marketing. Check destination coverage, quiet hours and subscriber-local time in the proposed sender. Assign an operator to STOP, HELP, support replies and balance disputes so an inbox migration does not strand customers.</p>
      <p>Inventory welcome, cart, VIP access, reward expiry, post-purchase and replenishment jobs. Mark each as retained, rebuilt or retired, with one production queue owner. Cancel old queued promotional sends and record their identifiers. Recheck purchase, stock, variant, price, code validity, support exclusions and permission at dispatch. Gift buyers and product users may need different replenishment treatment; VIP benefits should not silently become discount campaigns. Coordinate email education and urgent SMS through explicit collision rules suited to the promised frequency.</p>
      <h2>Economics without a fabricated winner</h2>
      <p>Price the same ordinary and peak workload: enabled sites, loyalty modules, profiles, destinations, SMS segments, MMS, carrier and number fees, staff work and support. Retaining loyalty while changing messaging can preserve cashier familiarity, but that benefit must be weighed against integration maintenance. Request a current quote for the actual boundary; no starting price or savings is established here.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical $100 order, $45 product cost and $10 fulfillment leave $45 before incentives and messaging. A $25 discount leaves $20. Twenty incremental orders at $20 less $300 campaign cost leave $100; thirty less $450 leave $150. A 25% discount alone does not establish a loss. Include reward liability and redemption costs without double counting the discount. Separate attributed orders from incremental orders, using an appropriate holdout where feasible, and inspect opt-outs by flow and full-price repeat purchases.</p>
      <h2>Accept the boundary, then release each job</h2>
      <p>Require a reconciled ledger, successful staff redemption, correct refund treatment and reviewed permission exceptions before activating benefit messages. Release each flow only after replay, withdrawal and purchase-exit cases pass. If a job fails, pause its delivery, preserve the authoritative ledger and reconcile fresh changes before retrying. A rollback must carry new withdrawals and redemptions; restoring an old export can revive a canceled promise.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before changing senders" />
</BaseLayout>
